1 OCCU Sergeant  162nd Regiment, New York Infantry Union Army 1861-1865
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.myheritage.se/research/collection-10331/usas-inbordeskrigsoldater-1861-1865?itemId=3464370-&action=showRecord&recordTitle=Frantz+Quisling
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/162nd_New_York_Infantry_Regiment
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Brief History
2 CONT The 162nd regiment organized in New York City, at Riker's Island, New York harbor, August 22 to October 18, 1862, under Colonel Lewis Benedict. It was authorized as one of the regiments of the Metropolitan Brigade and was known as
2 CONC  the 3rd Regiment, Metropolitan Guard.
2 CONT
2 CONT The regiment moved to Savannah, Georgia, June 30-July 7, 1865 and was mustered out October 12, 1865 under Colonel Blanchard, Lt. Colonel Van den Berg and Major Huxford. [1][2][3]
2 CONT
2 CONT For more information on the history of this unit, see:
2 CONT
2 CONT The Civil War Archive section, 162nd Regiment Infantry "3rd Metropolitan Guard", (accessed 22 August 2012).
2 CONT The New York State Military Museum website has a unit history project page for the 162nd Infantry Regiment.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Civil War in the East website gives a timeline for the involvement of 162nd New York Infantry Regiment in the war.
2 CONT
2 CONT Companies in this Regiment with the Counties of Origin
2 CONT Men often enlisted in a company recruited in the counties where they lived though not always. After many battles,companies might be combined because so many men were killed or wounded. However if you are unsure which company your 
2 CONC ancestor was in, try the company recruited in his county first.
2 CONT
2 CONT See the Regiment Roster, for listing of individuals, their company and their involvement.
2 CONT
2 CONT Company A - principally organized October 18th, 1862 in New York City
2 CONT
2 CONT Company B - principally organized October 14th, 1862 in New York City
2 CONT
2 CONT Company C - principally organized October 14th, 1862 in New York City
2 CONT
2 CONT Company D - principally organized October 18th, 1862 in New York City
2 CONT
2 CONT Company E - principally organized October 18th, 1862 in New York City
2 CONT
2 CONT Company F - principally organized August 22nd, 1862 in New York City [Companies D and K, 53d Infantry, second organization, were assigned to the regiment as Company F]
2 CONT
2 CONT Company G - principally organized October 18th, 1862 in New York City
2 CONT
2 CONT Company H - principally organized October 18th, 1862 in New York City
2 CONT
2 CONT Company I - principally organized October 18th, 1862 in New York City
2 CONT
2 CONT Company K - principally organized October 18th, 1862 in New York City
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Regimental History and Roster
2 CONT An Historical Sketch of the 162d Regiment N.Y. Vol. Infantry, by New York Infantry, Publisher Albany, NY : Weed, Parsons and Co., 1867, Online at:Internet Archive
2 CONT Source Material
2 CONT Beginning United States Civil War Research gives steps for finding information about a Civil War soldier. It covers the major records that should be used. Additional records are described in ‘New York in the Civil War’ and ‘United
2 CONC  States Civil War, 1861 to 1865’ (see below).
2 CONT National Park Service, The Civil War Soldiers and Sailors System, is searchable by soldier's name and state. It contains basic facts about soldiers on both sides of the Civil War, a list of regiments, descriptions of significant b
2 CONC attles, sources of the information, and suggestions for where to find additional information. 
2 CONT New York in the Civil War describes many Confederate and Union sources, specifically for New York, and how to find them.. These include compiled service records, pension records, rosters, cemetery records, Internet databases, publ
2 CONC ished books, etc. 
2 CONT United States Civil War, 1861 to 1865 describes and explains United States and Confederate States records, rather than state records, and how to find them. These include veterans’ censuses, compiled service records, pension record
2 CONC s, rosters, cemetery records, Internet databases, published books, etc. 
2 CONT Phisterer, Frederick. New York in the War of the Rebellion, 1861 to 1865. Albany, New York : J.B. Lyon, 1912. Google Books, other libraries with this book, FS Library Book 974.7 M2p; FS Library Film 1486494-6
2 CONT United States. Adjutant General's Office. Alphabetical index of the 162d New York Volunteer Infantry : being an abstract of the field and staff and company rolls. (Bethesda, Maryland : University Publications of America, c1992) FS
2 CONC  Library Fiche 6083858
2 CONT United States. Army. New York Infantry Regiment, 162nd. An historical sketch of the 162d Regiment N.Y. Vol. Infantry, (3d Metropolitan Guard), 19th Army Corps, 1862-1865. (Bethesda, Maryland : University Publications of America, c
2 CONC 1992)FS Library Fiche 6083857 and (Washington [District of Columbia] : Library of Congress, Photo duplication Service, 1988)FS Library Film 1486483
2 CONT The Union Army : a History of Military Affairs in the Loyal States, 1861-65, Records of the Regiments in the Union Army, Cyclopedia of Battles, Memoirs of Commanders and Soldiers, Reprint of original published: Madison, WI.: Feder
2 CONC al Pub. Co., 1908. Other libraries with this book,FS Library Books 973 M2ua, vols. 1-9
2 CONT "The Rikers: Their Island, Homes, Cemetery and Early Genealogy in Queens County, NY" by Edgar Alan Nutt, gives 8 pages of the book describing the island's use during the Civil War, particularly for the 162nd Regiment, New York Inf
2 CONC antry.
2 CONT Footnotes
2 CONT ↑ Frederick Henry Dyer, A Compendium of the War of the Rebellion, (Des Moines, Iowa: Dyer Publishing Co., 1908), as cited at Civil War Soldiers and Sailors System (database on-line) at https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/soldiers-and-sai
2 CONC lors-database.htm (accessed 2010).
2 CONT ↑ New York State Division of Military and Naval Affairs. "Unit History Project : 162nd Infantry Regiment” in New York State Military Museum and Veterans Research Center at http://dmna.state.ny.us/historic/reghist/civil/infantry/16
2 CONC 2ndInf/162ndInfMain.htm (accessed 27 December 2010) citing Frederick Phisterer, New York in the War of the Rebellion, 3rd ed. (Albany, N.Y.: J. B. Lyon Company, 1912).
2 CONT ↑ Hawks, Steve.The Civil War in the East. Database. http://www.civilwarintheeast.com/USA/NY/NY162.php (accessed 27 December 2010)
