2 CONT As we loved you, so we miss you; In our memory you are near. Loved, remembered, longed for always, Bringing many a silent tear. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jack delivered groceries, by bicycle, in and around Sunalta and Bankview, in the days of Calgary's streetcars. He attended the great sandstone schools where his father, J.T. North, Sr., was a principal. He took the train up to Sat
2 CONC urday night dances at the Banff Springs Hotel. He skiied into Skoki. Jack married Joan in a splendid ceremony in 1944 and they moved to Claresholm, where Jack’s mother, Clara  Amundsen, grew up. F/O North was a flying instructor a
2 CONC t No 15 R.C.A.F. Station Claresholm. He piloted some great planes through shearing Chinooks (sometimes backwards), and swapped many a tale at reunions. Jack donated some of his personal effects to the Claresholm & District Museum.
2 CONC  Jack spent a lot of time on the road, learning about people and their lives, usually selling filters for cars, trucks, and big machinery, and discovering every backroad in Western Canada. Jack built things like garages and fences
2 CONC , puttered around the cars, and recognized everyone he had ever meant, usually by name. Amazing!  Jack and Joan explored K-Country for many years, skiing, biking and hiking. Eventually, Jack bought a Lazy Boy chair.  Jack was our 
2 CONC miracle man, getting booted from the hospice after three months, then wheeling into Carewest Col. Belcher Care Centre to enjoy the "Out and Abouts" and the pubs. Not wanting a funeral service, Jack was toasted at a private gatheri
2 CONC ng. 
