1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ole, the first son born at Strand, was baptised on 29 December 1839 and confirmed at a formal ceremony in the Hvaler Church on 22 October 1854. He was issued with his seaman’s patent on 27 October 1863 but delayed 
2 CONC his first voyage until the following northern autumn.  On 3 September 1864 near the end of the normal sailing season he signed on the barque, Alma, at Fredrikshald for a short period of just 12 weeks. The following year, he was en
2 CONC gaged for the entire sailing season in the European trade from 6 April to 14 November as 1.styrmann (first mate) on board the barque, Columbus, of 231 n.r.t. Columbus would be his home at sea for the next 18 years interrupted by a
2 CONC  brief voyage to the United Kingdom with the schooner, Renen, in 1872.2 Ole’s future brother-in-law, Ole Anton Andersen, accompanied him as 2.styrmann (second mate) on board Columbus for the last three months of the 1866 season, w
2 CONC here its destination was the French port of Libourne.  A brief insight into the remuneration that Ole and the rest of the crew received on board Columbus can be gleaned from the account book for the year 1880. Ole heads the list o
2 CONC f men while the skipper, who in 1880 was M. Engebretsen, is the last of the personnel listed.  Ole was hired as 1.styrmann for the entire season comprising seven months and 25 days, as was the 2.styrmann, O. Amunsen; the tømmerman
2 CONC n (carpenter), M. Hansen; two lettmatroser (ordinary seamen); the kok (cook), M.J. Andreasen; and of course, the skiper [sic] (captain). The other crew members were engaged for shorter periods, such as two of the jungmenner (seame
2 CONC n) who each signed on for one voyage of one month and 22 days duration. Ole’s remuneration for the sailing season of seven months and 25 days at 64 kroner per month totalled 501.37 kroner and was only exceeded by the captain who r
2 CONC eceived 789.35 kroner at 100 kroner per month for the same period of sea service. In order to place Ole’s income in the context of the contemporary Norwegian economy, Ole had paid his mother-in-law, Anne Torine, 2 920 kroner for s
2 CONC ub-farm 1 under Stafsengen. As this transaction had occurred on 24 March 1879, a simple calculation indicates that Ole would have been able to discharge the potential debt after a period of almost five years and 10 months of equiv
2 CONC alent service in the merchant fleet. Realistically, this calculation excludes day-to-day living expenses but these were unlikely to be significant in the largely self-sufficient rural economy. Columbus was built in Mandal in 1844 
2 CONC and registered in Fredrikshald when it was sold to G. Mamen and John Christian Brenne in 1854. Brenne was originally from Kile farm on Kirkøy but he maintained Fredrikshald as the barque’s home port until he was joined by P. Anker
2 CONC  in 1862. In 1872 Columbus was sold to Niels and Christian Anker who continued to ply the European trade until it was sold to T. Torbjørnsen and others in 1882.3 The sale also coincided with Ole’s cessation of duties on this old s
2 CONC ailing vessel on 26 April 1882.  During his long service on board Columbus he visited ports as diverse as St. Nazaire, Rouen, Dieppe, Le Havre, Bordeaux, Gravelines and Calais in France, Emden in Germany as well as English, Dutch 
2 CONC and Baltic Sea ports. Columbus ultimately ran aground off the southern Dutch coast in early February 1889 with a cargo of lumber while en route from Fredrikstad to Penzance, England. Ole was not on board at the time. Prior to one 
2 CONC of the voyages of Columbus to Honfleur in France, Ole married Elisabeth Olava Andersdatter from Stafsengen farm (see page 95). The marriage ceremony took place on 5 February 1867 at the Hvaler Church when Ole was 27 and Elisabeth 
2 CONC Olava was 25 years of age. Ole then departed on 23 March in the capacity as 1.styrmann on board Columbus bound for France and continued sailing until the close of the season in early November. During his absence their first son, H
2 CONC ans Anton, was born just eight months after the marriage. This pattern of births prevailed for the remaining four children; all births occurred during the autumn and winter months following Ole’s shore break from the previous wint
2 CONC er season. Although Ole was qualified to perform as a captain on 6 January 1874, he signed on other vessels in the capacity of mate because of strong competition amongst the seafaring population for this prestigious position. Foll
2 CONC owing his long service on board Columbus he signed on Josephine at Sarpsborg for a three-week trip to Roskilde, Denmark. From March to November 1883, Ole was engaged as a crew member on board the Swedish built and Sarpsborg regist
2 CONC ered barque, Tellus. The Netherlands ports of Harlingen and Dordrecht were prominent destinations for the sea trade during 1884 on board the Prussian built schooner, Minerva, and on the same vessel in 1885 for ports in Scotland su
2 CONC ch as Bo’ness.4 By this stage the sailing days of Minerva were numbered and just two years later it was condemned in Lysekil, Sweden, while en route with a cargo of coal from Charlestown, United States of America to Svelvik, Norwa
2 CONC y. For the final two months of the 1885 sailing season, Ole signed on the Fredrikstad registered brig, Vigo, with Holland as its destination. Vigo was built in Tvedestrand on the southern coast of Norway in 1862 and was owned by t
2 CONC he large shipping firm Brødrene Ellefsen (Ellefsen Brothers) of Fredrikstad. The firm flourished from 1883 to the First World War and it was Vigo, the first vessel purchased by the brothers, that launched their successful business
2 CONC .5  Ole’s service on Vigo was broken by voyages on board the schooner, Fri, from 1 April to 25 May and the brig, Jenny, from 1 July to 2 September 1886. His 27 year career sailing the northern seas ended at Fredrikstad when he sig
2 CONC ned off Vigo for the last time on 10 October 1891 after having sailed as mate on the same vessel for the previous five sailing seasons to ports in England, Holland and more distant destinations (see Appendices). Ole was now in the
2 CONC  sixth decade of his life and concentrated on fishing for his livelihood. Occasionally, he and his son, Syver, were seen in the waters around Kirkøy in Lynn, a small, double-ended and open, clinker built snekke. It was bought for 
2 CONC Syver by his father mainly for pleasure but was also used for a little fishing. In the final years of his life, he lived at Skjærhalden with his daughter, Olette, and family. He died of a heart attack at Skjærhalden on 26 April 19
2 CONC 24. He was aged 84.
